  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor satellite imagery and analyzed synoptic \npattern indicate a strong low pressure system moving ashore in the\nPacific Northwest, an upper-level low centered over the MS/AL \nGulf coast. An upper-level high has closed over the Ohio River\nvalley after ridge sheared out. Easterly flow between these two \nfeatures will expand low-level humidity into our region, but \nactual conditions will remain dry into tomorrow apart from a few\npossible sprinkles as the low beings to lift northward.\n\nThe pattern Friday into the weekend still will be mostly quiet for\nour forecast area beneath southwest flow aloft. Interestingly\nenough, the upper-level low pressure system along the Gulf Coast\nis expected to initiate surface cyclogenesis in a baroclinic zone\nnear Florida on Friday. The National Hurricane Center highlights a\n40% chance for development into either a subtropical storm or\ntropical storm Friday into the weekend as the low moves northward\nalong the Atlantic coastline. No impacts are expected from this\nsystem in our forecast area, but it is one piece in the steering\nflow across the region. \n\nThe leading edge of the southern jet will nose over the Gulf \nCoast Sunday into Monday, as a weakening northern stream shortwave\ntrough passes. Rain chances are low (20-30%) at this time, but the\nwave and its cold front could squeeze out some moisture with\nshowers in the Sunday/Monday time frame. Confidence in the pattern\nheading into mid next week is still low, as a progressive \nnorthern stream and strong southern jet stream prevail over the\nCONUS. Temperature forecast is for \"near normal\" barring any \nsignificant systems. /NF/",
